Q: PHP Cast to my class A: <p>I did not see anyone suggest type narowwing via assert</p>
<p>This does not convert $object into ChildClass; it asserts that the object is already an instance of ChildClass.</p>
<p>This one may be bold, because assertions may be disabled.</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>ParentClass;
ChildClass &lt; ParentClass;


assert($child instanceof ChildClass);

$child // now it is ChildClass if originally was, or an exception is thrown
</code></pre>

Q: Drawing a simple 2D graph in java A: <p>I couldn't fetch that page (Stack Overflow is blocked for me), but &quot;drawing a simple 2D graph in Java&quot; is a common topic, so I'll give you a clean, working solution using Java's built-in Swing/Java2D — no external libraries needed.</p>
<h2>Approach</h2>
<p>We'll create a custom <code>JPanel</code> that overrides <code>paintComponent()</code> to draw axes and plot a line graph from an array of data points, scaled to fit the panel.</p>
<pre><code>import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class SimpleGraph extends JPanel {

    private List&lt;Double&gt; data;
    private final int PADDING = 40;
    private final int POINT_WIDTH = 6;

    public SimpleGraph(List&lt;Double&gt; data) {
        this.data = data;
        setPreferredSize(new Dimension(600, 400));
        setBackground(Color.WHITE);
    }

    @Override
    protected void paintComponent(Graphics g) {
        super.paintComponent(g);
        Graphics2D g2 = (Graphics2D) g;
        g2.setRenderingHint(RenderingHints.KEY_ANTIALIASING, RenderingHints.VALUE_ANTIALIAS_ON);

        int width = getWidth();
        int height = getHeight();

        double maxValue = data.stream().mapToDouble(d -&gt; d).max().orElse(1);
        double minValue = data.stream().mapToDouble(d -&gt; d).min().orElse(0);
        if (maxValue == minValue) maxValue = minValue + 1; // avoid divide-by-zero

        int graphWidth = width - 2 * PADDING;
        int graphHeight = height - 2 * PADDING;

        // Draw axes
        g2.setColor(Color.BLACK);
        g2.drawLine(PADDING, height - PADDING, width - PADDING, height - PADDING); // X axis
        g2.drawLine(PADDING, PADDING, PADDING, height - PADDING);                  // Y axis

        // Draw axis labels (min/max on Y)
        g2.drawString(String.format(&quot;%.1f&quot;, maxValue), 5, PADDING);
        g2.drawString(String.format(&quot;%.1f&quot;, minValue), 5, height - PADDING);

        // Compute point coordinates
        int[] xPoints = new int[data.size()];
        int[] yPoints = new int[data.size()];

        for (int i = 0; i &lt; data.size(); i++) {
            double x = PADDING + (i * (double) graphWidth / (data.size() - 1));
            double y = height - PADDING - ((data.get(i) - minValue) / (maxValue - minValue) * graphHeight);
            xPoints[i] = (int) x;
            yPoints[i] = (int) y;
        }

        // Draw connecting lines
        g2.setColor(Color.BLUE);
        g2.setStroke(new BasicStroke(2f));
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; xPoints.length - 1; i++) {
            g2.drawLine(xPoints[i], yPoints[i], xPoints[i + 1], yPoints[i + 1]);
        }

        // Draw points
        g2.setColor(Color.RED);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; xPoints.length; i++) {
            g2.fillOval(xPoints[i] - POINT_WIDTH / 2, yPoints[i] - POINT_WIDTH / 2, POINT_WIDTH, POINT_WIDTH);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List&lt;Double&gt; sampleData = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        double[] values = {5, 12, 8, 20, 15, 30, 22, 18, 25, 10};
        for (double v : values) sampleData.add(v);

        JFrame frame = new JFrame(&quot;Simple 2D Graph&quot;);
        frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        frame.add(new SimpleGraph(sampleData));
        frame.pack();
        frame.setLocationRelativeTo(null);
        frame.setVisible(true);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>How it works</h2>
<ol>
<li><p><strong><code>JPanel</code> subclass</strong> — <code>SimpleGraph</code> extends <code>JPanel</code> and overrides <code>paintComponent(Graphics g)</code>, which Swing calls automatically whenever the panel needs to redraw.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>Graphics2D</code></strong> — cast the <code>Graphics</code> object to <code>Graphics2D</code> for smoother lines (<code>setRenderingHint</code>) and stroke control.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Scaling</strong> — the data values are normalized against the min/max of the dataset so the graph always fits inside the panel, regardless of the data's actual range.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Axes</strong> — two simple lines mark the X and Y axes, with min/max labels on the Y-axis for reference.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Points and lines</strong> — each data value becomes an <code>(x, y)</code> pixel coordinate; consecutive points are connected with blue lines, and red dots mark each data point.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong><code>main</code> method</strong> — sets up a <code>JFrame</code>, adds the panel, and displays it — this is a runnable, self-contained example.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h2>To customize</h2>
<ul>
<li><p><strong>Multiple series</strong>: pass a <code>List&lt;List&lt;Double&gt;&gt;</code> and loop over each series with a different color.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Grid lines</strong>: add horizontal/vertical <code>drawLine</code> calls at regular intervals inside <code>paintComponent</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Bar chart instead</strong>: replace the line/point drawing with <code>g2.fillRect(...)</code> for each value.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Dynamic updates</strong>: call <code>repaint()</code> after modifying <code>data</code> (e.g., in a Swing <code>Timer</code>) to animate the graph.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Run it as-is and you'll get a window with a blue line graph plotting the sample values — swap in your own <code>List&lt;Double&gt;</code> to graph real data.</p>

Q: Download files from FTP if they are created within the last hour A: <p>Unfortunately, there's no really reliable and efficient way to retrieve modification timestamp of all files in a directory using features offered by .NET framework, as it does not support the FTP <code>MLSD</code>. The <code>MLSD</code> command provides a listing of remote directory in a standardized machine-readable format. The command and the format is standardized by <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3659/#section-7" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RFC 3659</a>.</p>
<p>Alternatives you can use, that are supported by .NET framework:</p>
<ul>
<li><p><code>ListDirectoryDetails</code> method (the FTP <code>LIST</code> command) to retrieve details of all files in a directory and then you deal with FTP server specific format of the details</p>
<p>DOS/Windows format: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/7060983/850848#39771146">C# class to parse WebRequestMethods.Ftp.ListDirectoryDetails FTP response</a><br />
*nix format: <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/1013486/850848#40045894">Parsing FtpWebRequest ListDirectoryDetails line</a></p>
</li>
<li><p><code>GetDateTimestamp</code> method (the FTP <code>MDTM</code> command) to individually retrieve timestamps for each file. An advantage is that the response is standardized by <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc3659/#section-3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">RFC 3659</a> to <code>YYYYMMDDHHMMSS[.sss]</code>. A disadvantage is that you have to send a separate request for each file, what can be quite inefficient. The file modification time is parsed for you into <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.net.ftpwebresponse.lastmodified" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>LastModified</code> property</a>:</p>
<pre><code>const string uri = &quot;ftp://example.com/remote/path/file.txt&quot;;
FtpWebRequest request = (FtpWebRequest)WebRequest.Create(uri);
request.Method = WebRequestMethods.Ftp.GetDateTimestamp;
FtpWebResponse response = (FtpWebResponse)request.GetResponse();
Console.WriteLine(&quot;{0} {1}&quot;, uri, response.LastModified);
if (response.LastModified &gt; DateTime.Now.Subtract(TimeSpan.FromHours(1)))
{
    // download
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p>Way easier is to use (I know you cannot) a 3rd party FTP client implementation that supports the modern <code>MLSD</code> command or that can directly download files given a time constraint.</p>
<p>For example <a href="https://winscp.net/eng/docs/library" rel="nofollow noreferrer">WinSCP .NET assembly</a> supports both <code>MLSD</code> and <a href="https://winscp.net/eng/docs/file_mask#size_time" rel="nofollow noreferrer">time constraints</a>.</p>
<p>There's even an example for your specific task: <a href="https://winscp.net/eng/docs/faq_script_modified_files#no_old_files" rel="nofollow noreferrer">How do I transfer new/modified files only?</a><br />
The example is for PowerShell, but translates to C# easily:</p>
<pre><code>// Setup session options
SessionOptions sessionOptions = new SessionOptions
{
    Protocol = Protocol.Ftp,
    HostName = &quot;ftp.example.com&quot;,
    UserName = &quot;username&quot;,
    Password = &quot;password&quot;,
};

using (Session session = new Session())
{
    // Connect
    session.Open(sessionOptions);

    // Download files modified with the last hour
    TransferOptions transferOptions = new TransferOptions();
    transferOptions.FileMask = &quot;*&gt;=1H&quot;;
    session.GetFiles(
        &quot;/remote/path/*&quot;, @&quot;C:\local\path\&quot;, false, transferOptions).Check();
}
</code></pre>
<p>See also syntax for <a href="https://winscp.net/eng/docs/file_mask#size_time" rel="nofollow noreferrer">time constraints</a>.</p>
<p><em>(I'm the author of WinSCP)</em></p>
